ne said it but I  know other people have said it as well  that it's really it's really chose kind  of mind-boggling and shocking to think  that we can more readily imagine the end  of a healthy earth than we can imagine  the end of our capitalist system our  current economic system and we say oh  it's terrible with climate change etc  but we don't say oh it's terrible our  economic system is generating this and  it absolutely is so we need to start to  think about what a different economic  system looks like and that that  corporate personhood corporate rights  references a call to do that and I think  we have a responsibility is advocates  not just to talk about rights of nature  and putting that into law because that  by itself isn't enough we also need to  say this is our positive vision we are  going to recognize our relationship with  nature and we're going to incorporate  that into everything we do but we can't  do that without taking away the  inhibitors the blockades the roadblocks  in our way and that's really this  economic system which fortunately  ecologically economists have started to  articulate other models that we can  start to use so if we combine that with  this rights of nature paradigm we can  start to have the beginnings of a new  type of governance system a new way of  self managing to recognize our  relationship with the earth so it's very  exciting to imagine this paradigm and I  think you've beautifully articulated why  it's so necessary to switch the paradigm  but I was wondering if you've also felt  that there are sometimes costs to  advancing an innovative framework  especially in the legal profession which  is based on precedent and tends to be  kind of more backwards-facing have you  found there to be professional or  political costs or push back on you  advancing a rates of nature model yeah  and I'll answer that but I feel bad  because I forgot to answer the last  little bit of your question where you  were talking about carbon taxes and tap  not really sure yeah so I apologize I'll  answer that quickly but to get at this  question of the economic system you know  if we folk what we've tended to do is  try to take the natural world which is  being very you know unruly  you know from an economic perspective  and not really fitting into our economic  models because it doesn't but we try to  monetize it to make it fit better and  that's what I think cap-and-trade is and  I accuse you know fishing quotas these  types of ways to monetize and trade the  environment is our solution to this this  economic system that is harming the  environment so we're creating a solution  out of the problem that you know that is  the genesis of what we're facing so I  think what we need to do and that's one  of the reasons that we especially as  environmental advocates need to address  the economic problem head-on is because  it's very easy to be led down this path  of all we need to do these other types  of activities in order to better  internalize the cost of the economic  system will never be able to do that we  can do that somewhat and we should you  know we should definitely do some of it  but to think that that's the solution is  is is misguided we need to address the  economic system head-on and that's the  real problem that we need to address so  on the political pushback question yeah  i mean a it's you do get people who say  oh well you know that's interesting you  know so or you know what do you think  about this this new water permit you  know and they just sort of flipped back  into the same you know regular  interaction so you know we don't really  have and I'm happy to weigh in and help  and try to push the ball forward in the  ways that I can but I think that you  know I think that people are starting to  listen I mean I do think there's been  pushed back but you don't tend to talk  to people who aren't willing at all to  listen because there's only so much time  in a day and you know you really only  need to talk to people who might be  interested in listening i have a friend  and the legislature who is a staffer who  does water work and a friend of mine  told me he'd said to him you know gosh  you know either I'm going crazy or she's  starting to make some sense you know so  you know it's it's something that's  slowly starting to take hold among  people who are interested in looking for  potential solutions and that's not to  say you know I have the answers the  rights of nature has all the answers  absolutely not you know there's there's  a lot of answers out there but  addressing I think the core issue of  addressing the problem in front of us  everybody agrees  that were not properly addressing the  problem in front of us because if we  were we wouldn't be facing climate  change in species extinctions and drying  waterways and all of the other threats  that we're facing to both in the  environment and to human rights  worldwide that we already know about so  people can agree there's a problem we're  not addressing it properly we're trying  a bunch of different things and at some  point maybe this will work so people are  starting to look for new solutions and  that's the reason that I put them out  there because I know the old ones aren't  going to work by themselves I want to  ask you a little bit about where you  found allies or not found allies in the  religious community as a Christian I was  raised with a deep understanding of  stewardship of nothing in creation  belonging to us but all belonging to god  and I'm very curious to see whether you  found that or whether you found any  pushback or have had no relationship  with the religious communities at all in  terms of the implementation or even just  the theological discussions about this  yeah no I mean I've found a number of  the folks that I work with are very you  know into the religious communities is a  big part of their lives and so a lot of  the people who tend to work on rights of  nature were very heavily influenced by  the writings of thomas berry perto de  seda who were catholic theologians and  gia loggia dei were very influential but  certainly not you know sort of this is  what the rules are and you must follow  me but you know these these principles  came out of there the recognition  especially the Thomas Berry had numb  about the spirituality of the earth  itself and I think that you know for  myself my own religious upbringing was  in the Catholic tradition and so I think  that that allowed me the space to sort  of not necessarily formally as you  articulated have the sense of  stewardship but have this more sense of  belonging you know and care and love and  I mean I think that that expression of  care and love that you get out of a  religious tradition can extend easily  out to and especially as children you  know I mean we could certainly easily  say it's children that you know we loved  a certain tree or we love to forest and  and that was natural and accepted but  can we say that now as adults it's  difficult especially in a work context  and why is that I mean that's important  to examine that question so for myself  the folks in who were working with  Thomas berry tended to be more in the  Catholic tradition there are a number of  Buddhist people that I've worked with  since that also are very heavily  interested in this work from their own  perspective of connection with the  larger universe with a larger your sense  of your larger interconnections we're  all one type of understanding and so I  think that all religious traditions at  their core have that essential  understanding you know I mean that's  where the golden rule is sort of you  know it runs across so many traditions  do onto others as you would have them do  unto you and that I think includes the  earth you know we need to be able to  think back to this question of  obligation you know what are we doing or  not doing you know that we would want  the earth to be doing or not