fornia  part of the problem is that people you  know in state government you know  regular people you know we and myself  included in other areas of my life you  know we've lost sense of our  responsibility for our own government we  need you know we have delegated a lot of  the responsibility and we say oh that  government is not doing such and such  you know but really it's all of our  responsibility every day to be able to  see how not only how our own actions  impact the natural world and each other  but how our government's actions and if  we don't like what they're doing if  we're not educated about that then that  I think that's that that's a real  problem that's a lack of exercise of our  obligation to the planet to be able to  do that so the fact that you know you  could say oh you know the constitution  of Ecuador says you know Nature has  rights but you know unless everybody you  know is is saying the same thing a lot  of people of Ecuador are and  unfortunately the government's not  really listening but unless everybody  sort of saying the same thing and trying  to articulate a new way of living then  we are again just sort of outsourcing  our responsibility to NGOs outsourcing  responsibility to non-existent  government entities that we think are  supposed to do the job but really  ultimately you know it's our job it's  all of our jobs and so we need to be  able to get back that obligation that  sense of responsibility we can we can't  outsource our own obligations for taking  care of the natural world something we  have to do every day so if if human  rights law sets it draws a boundary  between individuals and governments and  says basically we're saying where  government cannot infringe on an  individual's rights that seems like it's  a  it divides an individual from government  in some ways in terms of their freedom  would it be I know that the the full  intent of the rights of nature framework  is to help people understand that they  are totally mutually interdependent with  nature which is the opposite of dividing  humans from nature but wouldn't setting  up a rights of nature framework if it's  parallel to human rights framework  wouldn't that be dividing dividing with  a boundary between humans and nature  well I mean the way I understand your  question I think is Dalia cherelle  answer it anyway is you know this idea  of you know positive rights and negative  rights right so if you have fundamental  human rights and you draw a line between  the humans and the government you say  the government can't violate these  you're basically saying you know the  government can't throw me in jail you  know without you know do cause and you  know you can't like you know kill me you  know unless I have a fair trial or  whatever you know the government can't  affirmative Lee go and do things so you  have a protection from the government  for that whereas you know what some  people think of is nature's rights are  well they say well you know it's more  like the government has to go pay money  to restore ecosystems and species and  you know that maybe both the case so I  don't think it's inconsistent with human  rights or rights of nature to say that  the government can't infringe on your  fundamental rights I mean you are  separating the government but in that  way you're saying like I have a certain  worldview that I am part of the earth  that I have rights the earth has rights  and if you're coming in and infringing  on those rights i'm going to say no  that's my obligation to say no so saying  no is different from creating a  separation it's correcting an error in  terms of how we're supposed to behave  and that's all rights of nature is doing  is trying to allow us to create a  governance system system of law and  economics that shows us how to behave  and at some point we're going to get to  the point where the government needs to  you know because individually it's hard  to you know if we're going to restore  the California Bay Delta estuary we're  not all going to show up with a shovel  and say you know okay let's go you know  you need a plan you need a larger system  and that's where the government is  helpful but you need to build that  individually yourself  and say let's figure out how to do that  but fundamentally you you have to say if  the government you know is trying to  build for example twin tunnels to you  know take water through the Delta in  amounts that is unsustainable in order  to save us from ourselves then I think  that you it's time for you to say no  that that's that's the violation of  waterways right to flow and we need to  say no to that so I mean I think that  you can have a rights of nature  framework and say no at the same time  yeah I think we have time for about one  to two quick questions and one thing  that I think we found really interesting  was how in pursuing the rights of nature  paradigm we're caught you're creating  laws to shape norms and in law school  and other places I suppose we talk a lot  of it should norm shape laws or should  law shaped norms and I was curious how  you see that relationship and the rights  of nature contacts um I think I think  both are absolutely essential and if you  just look it I mean you know what we try  to do and it's it's all very new I'm a  but we try to do is look at other rights  based movements and see how they evolved  and see how they were created in a  vicodin you know throw out one book  reference there's a great book called  bury the chains which is a history of  the abolitionist movement maybe some of  your reddit in England the very first  they would call the very first  grassroots movement and you know the  first one have buttons and artwork and  you know and it was really interesting  because they were trying to change the  law you know to change the norms but  they also had to get a certain amount  and we've seen this with right space  movements in the states you have to get  a certain amount like if people sort of  on your side before you can start to  shift the tide and so they did that  through grassroots movements they did  that through lobbying you know  legislators are powerful people to get  them to say the right things they did  that through artwork they would draw  pictures of slave ships to illustrate to  people gosh look at what this looks like  you have no idea how horrible this is  how immoral this is and they were  successful in changing the law which  then you know people said oh gosh we  can't do that but once the law has  changed it starts to bring around other  people on the same thing with with gay  marriage you know you had to start to do  the  education the awareness even television  helped in that regard to make this the  understanding the norms change so that  you can change the law and I'm very  hopeful the Supreme Court is going to do  the right thing so you start to see that  you really need to be doing both and the  same is true with rights of nature I  mean I come at it from more of a legal  perspective because I'm a lawyer in a  policy perspective because I'm a policy  analyst but there are people that you  know do artwork there are people that  are doing music there are people that  are doing all kinds of other ways to  build awareness and build understanding  people doing you know religious studies  and retreats all of these things are  incredibly important to start to change  how people think and feel and that part  is really important how they feel about  the natural world and how they talk  about it even just linguistics you know  this idea about language is incredibly  important in terms of how we are able to  address these problems I was just  talking earlier about how a friend of  mine Michael McGonagle up at the  University of Victoria coined this term  that I like adjective noun problem and  so when you think about you know the  adjectives and announce that we use  sustainable development green economy  Natural Resources try to write a paper  about the environment without using the  word natural resources all of these  terms natural capital all these terms  the noun is the focus right so capital  development resources that's the  economic system that is controlling how  we think and how we do policy and how we  do law green natural sustainable all  these other terms they're the modifiers  they're the throwaways and and that's  what the natural world is really is a  throwaway in this larger economic  construct so what we need to do is we're  trying to change the norms but we also  have to change how we talk about it to  start with and you know I teach the when  I teach these classes I you know grant  downgrade people if they use the word  natural resources because it because  it's hard it's hard to do and and every  time you do it now I hope you think like  you know how would I think about this  differently how would I say this word  differently and is that changing my  construct of how i can create solutions  and everybody has something to offer to  this everybody yeah  that's great so thinking about these  students and we have a room full of  students here in the Divinity School in  the law school in the forestry school  for folks who might be going into this  field do you have advice for how to  utilize a rights of nature framework or  how to join the battle to shift the  paradigm I think my first advice would  be you from my own just from my own  perspective you know for me you know it  was a california environmental advocate  I you know did pretty well you know I  got some laws passed and and it was  working with really great colleagues on  some really great causes but for me I  could not make that leap to rights of  nature until I started having more of a  spiritual awakening until I started  meditating until I started you know  reading more spiritual literature of the  spirituality of the earth until I  started thinking about it in that way  that is not just the intellectual  rational type of fashion that we do as  lawyers and its policy analysts you know  the something I was used to  uncomfortable with it was not the way  that I was taught as engineering  undergrad until I started thinking about  the earth is something else that  something had its own spirituality that  I was intimately connected with it could  not make that leap and so I I would say  to people you know first you know just  put aside all the intellectual stuff and  just go for a hike you know go go sit in  a tree you know meditate do whatever it  is try to do it outside try to connect  back with the natural world and try to  reconnect with this feeling that I'm  sure we all had you know as children  about your connection with the earth and  I'll just if this is lost questions and  with a quick story I had to do a talk at  the California Environmental Law  associations to the California ABA  environmental groups annual meeting at  Yosemite a few years ago and Richard  love last child in the woods was there  speaking and we all got a copy of his  book and it was that was really nice and  and he said to all the lawyers works  were mostly compliance attorneys in  private practice and in law firms but  you know trying to do the right thing  which is good and enjoying the  conference and he said everybody don't  need to say right now but go talk to  people over the rest of the conference  think about you know when we were when  you were a child what was your  connection with nature what we  special connection with nature and I've  been to a lot of law conferences you  know but never like this where like  seriously for the next two or three days  I would hear strangers coming up over  the coffee pots and saying so you know  who's yours you know because mine was  fishing in this one creek with my dad  and I remember this one fall day and the  leaves were really pretty and and people  felt like they were open enough because  they'd been given that permission to  talk about their relationship with  nature in that way and until you sort of  open yourself up to that possibility  it's going to be really hard to do this  kind of work and I'd say just give  yourself permission to go out love the  earth and then you can bring that back  to your work on behalf of Yale Divinity  School forestry school and and the law  school just want to thank you again for  being with us today and thank all the  viewers for joining us thank you thank  you very much thanks so much
 

